<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-11-22T16:27:40Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Measles in America: Here's a constructive approach to beating the disease, based on Brazil's experience</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3a43caee3486b20400887b8334ee8ac941a98e3bde0d3a2364521fc4f3f34061</id><updated>2026-11-22T16:27:40Z</updated><published>2026-11-20T15:14:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/measles-in-america-here-s-a-constructive-approach-to-beating-the-disease-based-on-brazil-s-experienc/a-79990626--11abc405874e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/measles-in-america-here-s-a-constructive-approach-to-beating-the-disease-based-on-brazil-s-experience/a-79990626?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The US is now officially a measles contagion zone. By mid-2026, there were more infections than in all of the previous year. But the country could beat measles again, just like Brazil did two years ago.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>NASA's Voyager 1 reaches a light-day away from Earth</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4d7111f0084a2980cd6df105f74a225c7cc31a518d5f03d31467a89a20101479</id><updated>2026-11-22T16:27:40Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T17:24:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nasa-s-voyager-1-reaches-a-light-day-away-from-earth/a-79961817--190e9087abf2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nasa-s-voyager-1-reaches-a-light-day-away-from-earth/a-79961817?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">When just saying 'hello' takes a two-day round trip, you're a light-day, or 26 billion kilometers, from Earth. Only one human-made object has ever traveled that far — and its journey into interstellar space continues.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
